# Elixir for Gophers: Same Problems, Different Solutions

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"},
  {:makeup_syntect, "~> 0.1"},
  {:ecto_sqlite3, "~> 0.25"}
])

defmodule Slides do
  @root __DIR__

  @doc """
  Shows a Go source file with syntax highlighting. The path is relative
  to the repository root.

  Livebook does not highlight Go code blocks, so this function
  highlights the source with Makeup and shows it as HTML.

  ## Options

    * `:from` - show the source from the first line that starts with
      this text. Use it to hide the package clause and the imports.
  """
  def go_source(path, opts \\ []) do
    {lexer, lexer_opts} = Makeup.Registry.fetch_lexer_by_name!("go")

    source =
      @root
      |> Path.join(path)
      |> File.read!()
      |> drop_header(opts[:from])

    html = Makeup.highlight(source, lexer: lexer, lexer_options: lexer_opts)
    css = Makeup.Styles.HTML.StyleMap.one_dark_style() |> Makeup.stylesheet()

    Kino.HTML.new("""
    <style>
      #{css}
      body { margin: 0; }
      .highlight {
        margin: 0;
        padding: 16px;
        border-radius: 8px;
        font-family: "JetBrains Mono", monospace;
        font-size: 14px;
        line-height: 1.5;
        tab-size: 4;
        overflow-x: auto;
      }
    </style>
    #{html}
    """)
  end

  @doc """
  Builds the Go program in `go/<name>` and runs it once.
  Shows the output and the exit status.

  ## Options

    * `:race` - build the program with the race detector (`-race`)
  """
  def go_run(name, opts \\ []) do
    {output, status} = name |> go_build(opts) |> System.cmd([], stderr_to_stdout: true)
    Kino.Text.new(output <> "\nexit status #{status}", terminal: true)
  end

  @doc """
  Builds the Go program in `go/<name>` and returns the path of the binary.
  Takes the same options as `go_run/2`.
  """
  def go_build(name, opts \\ []) do
    go = System.find_executable("go") || raise "go is not in PATH"
    race = Keyword.get(opts, :race, false)
    bin = System.tmp_dir!() |> Path.join("elixir_for_gophers_#{name}#{if race, do: "_race"}")
    flags = if race, do: ["-race"], else: []
    args = ["build", "-trimpath"] ++ flags ++ ["-o", bin, "./go/#{name}"]

    case System.cmd(go, args, cd: @root, stderr_to_stdout: true) do
      {_output, 0} -> bin
      {output, _status} -> raise "go build failed:\n#{output}"
    end
  end

  # Without `from`, removes only the `//go:build` line of a snippet.
  defp drop_header(source, nil), do: String.replace(source, ~r/\A\/\/go:build[^\n]*\n+/, "")

  defp drop_header(source, from) do
    [_header, rest] = String.split(source, "\n" <> from, parts: 2)
    from <> rest
  end
end
```

## Elixir for Gophers

### Same problems, different solutions

Nikola Begedin · V7

## Same problems, different solutions

1. Language features
2. Runtime
3. Typical web project
4. Types, project shapes
5. An example of a real codebase

## Where we meet

* **Go:** *Go Developer Survey 2025* 74% build CLIs, 73% build API services, 46% build websites and 38% build cloud infrastructure tools.
* **Elixir:** *2025 Elixir community survey* 97% use Phoenix (web + API), 86% use LiveView.
* **Where we meet?** Web API.

#### Similarities

| Go                     | Elixir                              |
| ---------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| `go fmt`               | `mix format`                        |
| `go test`              | `mix test` (ExUnit)                 |
| `go doc`, pkg.go.dev   | `h/1` in IEx, ExDoc and HexDocs     |
| `go mod`, `go get`     | `mix deps.get`, Hex                 |
| `if err != nil`        | `{:ok, value}` / `{:error, reason}` |
| structs and interfaces | structs, protocols and behaviours   |

Small languages. No classes. No inheritance.

## Language: Pattern matching

**Elixir:** `=` is a match. The left side is a pattern, and the match binds the variables in it.

```elixir
response = {:ok, %{name: "Ana", langs: ["Go", "Elixir"]}}

{:ok, %{name: name, langs: [first | _rest]}} = response
{name, first}
```

```elixir
{:ok, %{name: "Ana", langs: ["Go", second]}} = response
second
```

```elixir
try do
  {:ok, %{name: "Annie"}} = response
rescue
  error in MatchError -> Exception.format(:error, error) |> Kino.Text.new(terminal: true)
end
```

**Elixir:** `case` runs the first branch whose pattern matches.

```elixir
users = %{1 => "Ana"}

for id <- [1, 2] do
  case Map.fetch(users, id) do
    {:ok, name} -> "user #{id}: #{name}"
    :error -> "user #{id}: not found"
  end
end
```

* A value that does not match any pattern raises `MatchError`. In a process, that is a crash.
* A case with a missing clause -> `CaseClauseError`
* `receive`, `with` and function clauses use the same patterns.

## Function clauses and pipes

**Go:** one function with a `switch` inside.

**Elixir:** one function with many clauses (function heads). The first clause whose pattern and guard (`when`) match is the one that runs.

```elixir
defmodule Status do
  def text(200), do: "OK"
  def text(404), do: "Not Found"
  def text(code) when code in 500..599, do: "Server Error"
  def text(_code), do: "Unknown"
end

for code <- [200, 404, 503, 302], do: {code, Status.text(code)}
```

* `def text(_code)` - catch-all clause
* without it -> `FunctionClauseError`

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

* `|>` is the pipe operator. Takes output of left function, passes as first argument into right
  function.
* allows nested function calls to be written left to right or top to bottom

```elixir
title = "  elixir for gophers  "

# words = String.split(String.upcase(String.trim(title)))

words =
  title
  |> String.trim()
  |> String.upcase()
  |> String.split()

# can also be 
# words = title |> String.trim() |> String.upcase() |> String.split()

{title, words}
```

## Processes vs goroutines

Neither is an OS thread or an OS process. Both runtimes run many of them on a few OS threads.

|                | Go: goroutines, CSP (Hoare, 1978) | BEAM: processes, actors (Hewitt, 1973) |
| -------------- | --------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |
| The address    | none: you talk to a channel       | a PID, with a mailbox                  |
| Memory         | one shared heap                   | own heap and own GC                    |
| Send           | waits for a receiver              | copies the message, does not wait      |
| Receive        | one channel, or `select`          | own mailbox, by pattern                |
| Backpressure   | yes: a full channel blocks        | no: a mailbox can grow                 |
| Failure        | not in the model                  | links and monitors                     |
| Other machines | no                                | yes, with the same `send/2`            |

* **Myth:** "Go scheduling is cooperative." Since Go 1.14, the runtime also stops a goroutine in a tight loop, after about 10 ms.
* **Myth:** "Elixir processes are insanely cheap." Cheap compared with an OS thread, but both cost about 2.6 KB. The difference is isolation.
* "Share memory by communicating" is a Go proverb, but a mutex is also normal Go.

Go has backpressure built in. The BEAM has addresses, crash signals and distribution built in.

## Processes vs goroutines

**Go:** start 100,000 goroutines that wait on a channel.

```elixir
Slides.go_source("go/goroutines/main.go", from: "const count")
```

```elixir
Slides.go_run("goroutines")
```

**Elixir:** start 100,000 processes that wait for a message.

```elixir
defmodule Spawner do
  def start(count) do
    for _ <- 1..count do
      spawn(fn ->
        receive do
          :stop -> :ok
        end
      end)
    end
  end
end

before = :erlang.memory(:processes)
{ms, pids} = :timer.tc(fn -> Spawner.start(100_000) end, :millisecond)
used = :erlang.memory(:processes) - before
Enum.each(pids, &send(&1, :stop))

Kino.Text.new(
  """
  processes: 100000
  time: #{ms} ms
  bytes per process: #{div(used, 100_000)}
  """,
  terminal: true
)
```

**Go:** a goroutine has no address. You talk to a channel.

```elixir
Slides.go_source("go/channels/main.go", from: "func main")
```

```elixir
Slides.go_run("channels")
```

**Elixir:** a process has an address. You send a message to its PID.

```elixir
pong =
  spawn(fn ->
    Process.set_label(:pong)

    receive do
      {:ping, from} -> send(from, :pong)
    end

    receive do
      :stop -> :ok
    end
  end)

{:ok, diagram} =
  Kino.Process.seq_trace(fn ->
    send(pong, {:ping, self()})

    receive do
      :pong -> :ok
    end
  end)

send(pong, :stop)
diagram
```

Both runtimes start 100,000 tasks in milliseconds. A PID can get messages from any process, also on another node.

## Fan out, time out and cancel

Check 100 links, 10 at a time. One link in ten hangs. Stop the wait after 500 ms.

**Go:** a semaphore channel limits the checks. `context.WithTimeout` stops the wait.

```elixir
Slides.go_source("go/fanout/main.go", from: "func main")
```

```elixir
Slides.go_run("fanout")
```

**Elixir:** `Task.async_stream/3` limits the tasks and stops a slow task.

```elixir
check = fn link ->
  if rem(link, 10) == 0, do: Process.sleep(:infinity)
  :rand.uniform(200) |> Process.sleep()
end

{ms, results} =
  :timer.tc(
    fn ->
      0..99
      |> Task.async_stream(check, max_concurrency: 10, timeout: 500, on_timeout: :kill_task)
      |> Enum.frequencies_by(fn
        {:ok, _} -> :ok
        {:exit, :timeout} -> :timeout
      end)
    end,
    :millisecond
  )

Map.put(results, :ms, ms)
```

* **Go:** you give `ctx` to every function, and `check` must watch `ctx.Done()`. A function that ignores `ctx` continues after the timeout or `cancel()`. Go 1.27 adds a goroutine leak profile to find these goroutines.
* **Elixir:** there is no `ctx` argument, and `check` knows nothing about timeouts. `Task.async_stream/3` kills a task that is too slow. `Task.shutdown/2` or `Process.exit/2` stops any process, wherever its code is.
* Links send the stop to the child processes too.

The code size is similar. Elixir can stop any work from outside. Go makes cancellation visible in each signature.

## Errors: same idea, different crash

Expected errors are values in both languages.

**Go:** each step checks and wraps the error.

```elixir
Slides.go_source("go/snippets/errors.go", from: "// Expected errors")
```

**Elixir:** the same idea with `with`.

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
def load_user(id) do
  with {:ok, row} <- Db.query_user(id),
       {:ok, user} <- parse_user(row) do
    {:ok, user}
  end
end

# if DB.query_user(1) returns {:error, :not_found}, 
# load_user(1) returns {:error, :not_found}
# if parse_user(row) returns :invalid_row
# load_user(id) returns :invalid_row
```

Unexpected errors are different.

**Go:** a panic in one goroutine stops the whole program.

```elixir
Slides.go_source("go/crash/main.go", from: "func main")
```

```elixir
Slides.go_run("crash")
```

**Elixir:** a crash stops one process. A monitor tells you why.

```elixir
for id <- 1..3 do
  spawn_monitor(fn ->
    Process.sleep(id * 100)
    if id == 2, do: raise("boom")
    IO.puts("worker #{id} done")
  end)
end

reasons =
  for _ <- 1..3 do
    receive do
      {:DOWN, _ref, :process, _pid, :normal} -> :normal
      {:DOWN, _ref, :process, _pid, {error, _stack}} -> {:crashed, Exception.message(error)}
    end
  end

IO.puts("cell: still running")
reasons
```

**Caveat:** `net/http` recovers a panic in a handler. A panic in a goroutine that the handler starts still stops the server.

For unexpected errors, Elixir stops one process and Go stops the program.

## Let it crash, with a supervisor

* A supervisor starts child processes and restarts them when they crash.
* Strategies:
  * `:one_for_one` restarts only the crashed child. Use it when the children are independent.
  * `:rest_for_one` restarts the crashed child and all children that started after it. Use it when later children depend on earlier ones.
  * `:one_for_all` restarts all children. Use it when the children work only as a group.
* Too many crashes in a short time: the supervisor stops too, and its own supervisor decides.
  * Goes all the way up to the application supervisor and application crashes - common trap for those new to Elixir
* **Go:** a `recover` loop around each goroutine, or Kubernetes restarts the whole program.

**Elixir:** a supervisor with three workers.

```elixir
defmodule Worker do
  use GenServer

  def start_link(id), do: GenServer.start_link(__MODULE__, id)

  @impl true
  def init(id), do: {:ok, %{id: id, started_at: Time.utc_now()}}
end

defmodule Workers do
  use Supervisor

  def start_link(_), do: Supervisor.start_link(__MODULE__, :ok, name: __MODULE__)

  @impl true
  def init(:ok) do
    children = for id <- 1..3, do: Supervisor.child_spec({Worker, id}, id: id)
    Supervisor.init(children, strategy: :one_for_one)
  end
end

Kino.start_child(Workers)
Kino.Process.sup_tree(Workers)
```

Kill worker 2. The supervisor starts a new worker 2 with a new PID.

```elixir
{2, pid, :worker, _} = Workers |> Supervisor.which_children() |> List.keyfind(2, 0)
Process.exit(pid, :kill)
Process.sleep(100)

Kino.Layout.grid([
  Kino.Text.new("killed #{inspect(pid)}"),
  Kino.Process.sup_tree(Workers)
])
```

Elixir restarts small parts of a program. Go usually restarts the whole program, with help from the platform.

## State without locks

100 callers increment a counter at the same time, 1,000 times each.

**Go:** shared memory. One counter has a mutex, the other counter has no lock.

<!-- livebook:{"reevaluate_automatically":true} -->

```elixir
Slides.go_source("go/counter/main.go", from: "// Counter is safe")
```

```elixir
Slides.go_run("counter")
```

**Go:** the same program, built with `-race`. The race detector finds the missing lock.

```elixir
Slides.go_run("counter", race: true)
```

**Elixir:** the state is inside one process.

```elixir
defmodule Counter do
  use GenServer

  def start_link(_), do: GenServer.start_link(__MODULE__, 0, name: __MODULE__)
  def increment, do: GenServer.call(__MODULE__, :increment)
  def value, do: GenServer.call(__MODULE__, :value)

  @impl true
  def init(n), do: {:ok, n}

  @impl true
  def handle_call(:increment, _from, n), do: {:reply, :ok, n + 1}
  def handle_call(:value, _from, n), do: {:reply, n, n}
end

Kino.start_child(Counter)

1..100
|> Task.async_stream(fn _ -> for _ <- 1..1_000, do: Counter.increment() end, max_concurrency: 100)
|> Stream.run()

Counter.value()
```

* **Go:** without the mutex, increments get lost. `-race` finds a race only when it occurs during the run.
* **Elixir:** the process handles one message at a time, so it needs no lock.
* For many concurrent readers, use ETS: an in-memory table with concurrent reads.

A Go mutex is faster for hot shared memory. Elixir has no data races, but logic races are still possible.

## Many nodes, one system

* Distributed Erlang connects BEAM nodes. `send/2` works the same for a PID on another node.
* Phoenix.PubSub, Channels and LiveView use this to send events to every node in a cluster.
* **Go:** gRPC, NATS or Redis between services, plus service discovery.

Start two more nodes on this laptop.

```elixir
start_node = fn prefix ->
  {:ok, peer, node} =
    :peer.start_link(%{
      # A unique name, so the cell can run again while old nodes still run
      name: :peer.random_name(prefix),
      host: ~c"127.0.0.1",
      longnames: true,
      connection: :standard_io,
      exec: :code.root_dir() |> Path.join("bin/erl") |> String.to_charlist(),
      args: [~c"-setcookie", Node.get_cookie() |> Atom.to_charlist()]
    })

  :ok = :peer.call(peer, :code, :add_paths, [:code.get_path()])
  true = Node.connect(node)
  {peer, node}
end

{alpha_peer, alpha} = start_node.(:alpha)
{beta_peer, beta} = start_node.(:beta)
[alpha, beta]
```

Start a process on `beta` and send a message to it.

```elixir
pid =
  Node.spawn(beta, fn ->
    receive do
      {:hello, from} -> send(from, {:hello_back, node()})
    end
  end)

send(pid, {:hello, self()})

receive do
  reply -> reply
after
  1_000 -> :timeout
end
```

Connect `alpha` to `beta`, then stop both nodes.

```elixir
true = :erpc.call(alpha, Node, :connect, [beta])
nodes_seen_by_alpha = :erpc.call(alpha, Node, :list, [])

:peer.stop(alpha_peer)
:peer.stop(beta_peer)
nodes_seen_by_alpha
```

Elixir has clustering built in, for one trusted system. Go with gRPC is better across languages and trust boundaries.

## Look inside a running system

Go has good tools for CPU and memory profiling. Elixir lets you look inside a live system and change it.

* In production, `bin/my_app remote` opens an IEx shell inside the running release.
* Phoenix LiveDashboard and Oban Web show the same data in a browser.
* You can read the state of any process and trace its messages, with no restart.
* **Go:** pprof, `runtime/trace` with the flight recorder (Go 1.25), the goroutine leak profile (Go 1.27).

Read the state of worker 1 from the supervisor slide.

```elixir
{1, pid, _, _} = Workers |> Supervisor.which_children() |> List.keyfind(1, 0)

%{
  state: :sys.get_state(pid),
  info: Process.info(pid, [:memory, :message_queue_len, :reductions]),
  processes_on_this_node: Process.list() |> length()
}
```

## A web project: Gin and Phoenix

Go lets each team select its layout. Phoenix gives all projects the same layout, so a new developer knows where to look.

* **Go:** Gin is the most used Go web framework (48%). Many teams use only the standard library `net/http` (32%). *JetBrains, Go ecosystem surveys 2025*
* **Elixir:** `mix phx.new my_app` generates a Phoenix project.

|                | Go with Gin (a common layout)    | Phoenix 1.8                               |
| -------------- | -------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |
| Start          | `cmd/api/main.go`                | `lib/my_app/application.ex`, a supervisor |
| Routes         | `r.GET("/users/:id", h.GetUser)` | `get "/users/:id", UserController, :show` |
| Handlers       | `internal/handler/`              | `lib/my_app_web/controllers/`             |
| Business logic | `internal/service/`              | `lib/my_app/accounts.ex`, a context       |
| Database       | `internal/store/` with sqlc      | `lib/my_app/repo.ex` and Ecto schemas     |

* **Go:** the Go team documents only `cmd/` and `internal/`. Each team selects the rest.
* **Elixir:** `my_app` holds the business logic, and `my_app_web` holds the HTTP code. The generators add code in the same places.

## Talking to the database

**Go:**

* `database/sql` has a connection pool built in. pgx is the usual Postgres driver.
* sqlc generates typed Go code from SQL. GORM is the popular ORM. sqlx scans rows into structs.
* Migrations: goose, golang-migrate or Atlas.

```elixir
Slides.go_source("go/snippets/sqlc.go", from: "// sqlc reads")
```

**Elixir:**

* Ecto has four parts: Repo, Schema, Changeset and Query. Postgrex is the driver. DBConnection is the pool.
* Ecto is not an ORM. A schema is a struct, not an object. There is no lazy loading: you preload associations.
* Migrations are part of Ecto (`mix ecto.migrate`).

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
def list_active_users(since) do
  from(u in User,
    where: u.active and u.inserted_at > ^since,
    order_by: u.name,
    select: [:id, :name, :email]
  )
  |> Repo.all()
end
```

sqlc checks your SQL when you generate the code. Ecto queries compose, and validation errors are data.

## Ecto in action

A schema, a changeset and a SQLite database in a temporary file.

```elixir
defmodule Demo.Repo do
  use Ecto.Repo, otp_app: :demo, adapter: Ecto.Adapters.SQLite3
end

defmodule Demo.User do
  use Ecto.Schema
  import Ecto.Changeset

  schema "users" do
    field :name, :string
    field :email, :string
    field :active, :boolean, default: true
    timestamps()
  end

  def changeset(user, attrs) do
    user
    |> cast(attrs, [:name, :email, :active])
    |> validate_required([:name, :email])
    |> validate_format(:email, ~r/@/)
  end
end

database = System.tmp_dir!() |> Path.join("elixir_for_gophers.db")
File.rm(database)
Kino.start_child({Demo.Repo, database: database, pool_size: 1, log: false})

Demo.Repo.query!("""
CREATE TABLE users (
  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
  name TEXT, email TEXT, active BOOLEAN,
  inserted_at TEXT, updated_at TEXT
)
""")
```

A transaction is all or nothing. The second insert fails, so the first insert does not stay. Validation errors are data, not exceptions.

```elixir
alias Demo.{Repo, User}

eve = %User{} |> User.changeset(%{name: "Eve", email: "eve@example.com"})
mal = %User{} |> User.changeset(%{name: "Mal", email: "no email"})

{:error, failed_step, changeset, _changes_so_far} =
  Ecto.Multi.new()
  |> Ecto.Multi.insert(:eve, eve)
  |> Ecto.Multi.insert(:mal, mal)
  |> Repo.transact()

%{failed_step: failed_step, errors: changeset.errors, users_in_db: Repo.aggregate(User, :count)}
```

* Oban can put a job in the same `Ecto.Multi`, so the job exists only if the transaction commits. This works only when Oban uses the same repo as the data. River (Go) does the same with `InsertTx`.
* The SQL Sandbox gives each test its own transaction, so tests that use Postgres can run with `async: true`.

## Go types and Elixir types

| Go                  | Elixir                                                              |
| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `int64`, `float64`  | integer with no size limit, float                                   |
| `string`            | string: a UTF-8 binary                                              |
| `[]T`               | list (a linked list), or tuple (fixed size)                         |
| `map[K]V`           | map, with keys and values of any type                               |
| `struct`            | struct: a map with fixed keys                                       |
| `const` with `iota` | atoms: `:active`, `:banned`                                         |
| `error`, `nil`      | `{:error, reason}`. `nil` is an atom                                |
| pointers            | none: all data is immutable                                         |
| interfaces          | protocols (for data) and behaviours (for modules, like `GenServer`) |

* **Go:** a variable has a type. The compiler checks every assignment and every call.
* **Elixir:** a value has a type, but a variable does not. The same variable can hold an integer or a string.
* An integer does not overflow. `2 ** 100` gives the exact value.

## Type checking

* **Go:** static types and generics (generic methods since Go 1.27). The compiler rejects a program with a type error.
* **Elixir:** gradual types. The compiler infers types from patterns and guards, with no annotations.

**Elixir 1.20:** the compiler finds the type error in this module.

```elixir
defmodule Shapes do
  def area({:square, side}), do: side * side
  def area({:circle, radius}), do: 3.14 * radius * radius

  def describe(shape), do: "Area: " <> area(shape)
end
```

## Typespecs and Dialyzer

* **Go:** the types are part of the code, and the compiler checks them. Go needs no second tool.
* **Elixir:** `@type` and `@spec` describe the types of a function. They are optional.

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
@type shape :: {:square, number()} | {:circle, number()}

@spec area(shape()) :: number()
def area({:square, side}), do: side * side
```

* Dialyzer (`mix dialyzer` with the dialyxir package) reads the typespecs. It reports only code that can never work, so it misses many bugs.
* On a large project, Dialyzer is slow, and its messages are hard to read.
* The Elixir 1.20 compiler does not read `@spec`. It infers its own types.

Go gives stronger guarantees today. Elixir finds more bugs with each release, with no change to your code.

## Project shapes and the right tool

| Go is the better choice for                             | Elixir is the better choice for                         |
| ------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
| CLIs and developer tools: one static binary, fast start | Many long-lived connections: chat, presence, WebSockets |
| Infrastructure: Kubernetes operators, proxies, agents   | Real-time features and steady latency under load        |
| CPU-heavy work and tight memory limits                  | Systems that must stay up when parts fail               |
| Many small services on a platform                       | One product codebase with few moving parts              |

## Use both: Ports

A Port starts an OS program and connects to its stdin and stdout. Go does the CPU work. Elixir owns the OS process.

```elixir
Slides.go_source("go/port/main.go", from: "func main")
```

Run it once with `System.cmd`. The program starts, counts and stops.

```elixir
bin = Slides.go_build("port")
{output, 0} = System.cmd(bin, ["10", "1000", "10000000"])
String.split(output)
```

A Port keeps the program running. Send a number, receive the count of primes below it.

```elixir
port = Port.open({:spawn_executable, bin}, [:binary, :exit_status, line: 1024])

count_primes = fn n ->
  Port.command(port, "#{n}\n")

  receive do
    {^port, {:data, {:eol, line}}} -> String.to_integer(line)
  after
    5_000 -> raise "no reply from Go"
  end
end

%{
  os_pid: Port.info(port, :os_pid),
  counts: for(n <- [10, 1_000, 10_000_000], do: {n, count_primes.(n)})
}
```

Close the Port. Go reads the end of stdin and stops.

```elixir
Port.close(port)
```

## go-backend: an Elixir app called go-backend

* One Phoenix app, not an umbrella: about 5,000 modules and 550,000 lines in `lib/`, about 100 contexts.
* About 1,800 test files. 70% of them run with `async: true`.
* About 150 Oban workers, 11 Ecto repos, about 1,200 migrations.
* Other languages in the BEAM: a Rust NIF for text processing, and Python through Snex.
* One release with four node roles (`:api`, `:core`, `:worker`, `:reporting`) in one cluster.
* Only 16 hand-written GenServers. Phoenix, Oban and Broadway own most processes.

## Boundaries in a big codebase

* Elixir has no `internal/` packages. Any module can call any other module.
* Boundary adds this check to the compiler. Each boundary lists its `deps` and `exports`. A call outside these rules is a compiler warning.
* go-backend has 147 boundaries. `dirty_xrefs` lists the known exceptions in old code.
* **Go:** `internal/` and the rule against import cycles are part of the toolchain.

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
defmodule AgiDb.Organizations do
  use Boundary,
    deps: [AgiDb.Billing, AgiDb.Events, AgiDb.IAM, AgiDb.Repo, AgiDb.Workspaces, ...],
    exports: [Organization, Commands.CreateOrganization, Events.OrganizationWorkspaceAdded, ...]

  use AgiDb.Knigge,
    otp_app: :agidb,
    config_key: :organizations,
    default: AgiDb.Organizations.Internal.OrganizationsImpl

  @callback create_organization(Commands.CreateOrganization.t()) :: ...
end
```

* Each context is a facade: `@callback`s plus Knigge. Knigge sends each call to the implementation from the config.
* Tests replace the implementation with a Hammox mock (Mox with typespec checks). go-backend has 119 facades.
* Knigge's last release is from 2021. go-backend wraps it to remove a compile-time dependency.

## Compile times

* **Go:** a fast compiler and the build cache are design goals. Unused imports are errors.
* **Elixir:** modules depend on each other at compile time, through exports, or at runtime. A change recompiles the compile-time dependents.
* Macros and `use` add compile-time dependencies. One small change can recompile half of a big app.
* Tools: `mix xref graph --label compile-connected`, `mix xref trace`, and `--fail-above` for CI.
* Elixir 1.19 compiles large projects up to 4 times faster. Elixir 1.20 is faster again on many cores.

go-backend checks this in CI.

```bash
# No more than 10 compile-connected cycles
mix xref graph --format cycles --label compile-connected --fail-above 10

# definitions.ex files must not depend on other project modules
mix xref graph --source lib/agidb/.../definitions.ex --fail-above 0
```

## Takeaways

* Same problems: concurrency, failures, state, data.
* Go puts the solutions in your code and in the platform. Elixir puts them in the runtime.
* Choose Go for tools, infrastructure and CPU-heavy services.
* Choose Elixir for connected, real-time products that must stay up when parts fail.
* You can use both, through a Port, a NIF or a network call.

Slides and code: [github.com/begedin/elixir-for-gophers](https://github.com/begedin/elixir-for-gophers)

## Questions

## Backup: CPU speed

The same sieve of Eratosthenes, for the numbers below 10,000,000, on an Apple M3 Pro.

|                                                         | Time         |
| ------------------------------------------------------- | ------------ |
| Go                                                      | 17 ms        |
| Elixir: a compiled module, the JIT, an `:atomics` array | about 440 ms |

* The BEAM is slow for tight loops over numbers, also with the JIT (since OTP 24).
* For CPU-heavy work, use a Port (like the Go program in this talk), a NIF in Rust with Rustler, or Nx for numerical work.
* Most web backends wait for the database and the network, not for the CPU.

## Backup: Deployment

|               | Go                                | Elixir                                                    |
| ------------- | --------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |
| Artifact      | one static binary                 | a release (`mix release`): your code and the runtime      |
| Minimal size  | 5.2 MB, with `net/http`, stripped | 16 MB                                                     |
| Start         | less than 10 ms                   | about 0.25 s for the VM, more for a Phoenix app           |
| Cross-compile | yes: `GOOS` and `GOARCH`          | no: build on the target OS and CPU, usually in Docker     |
| Cluster       | not necessary                     | DNSCluster (in Phoenix 1.8) or libcluster finds the nodes |

* Measured on this laptop, with a new `mix new --sup` project and a small Go program.
* `mix phx.gen.release --docker` generates a release and a Dockerfile for a Phoenix app.
* Both run well on Kubernetes.

## Backup: Hiring and the ecosystem

|                       | Go                                                   | Elixir                                                      |
| --------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
| Used in the last year | 16.4% of the respondents                             | 2.7% of the respondents                                     |
| Admired               | 56.5%: 8th of 42 languages                           | 65.9%: 3rd, after Rust and Gleam                            |
| Packages              | no central registry: each Git repository is a module | Hex: about 26,700 packages                                  |
| Cloud SDKs            | official SDKs from AWS and Kubernetes                | community packages (ExAws, k8s)                             |
| Hiring                | a large pool of developers                           | a small pool: hire from other languages and teach Elixir    |
| Static types          | in the language                                      | gradual types in Elixir, or Gleam: static types on the BEAM |

* Source for the first two rows: Stack Overflow Developer Survey 2025.
* "Admired": the users of a language who want to use it again next year.

## Backup: Hot code upgrades

* The BEAM can load a new version of a module into a running system. The old and the new version can run at the same time.
* A full hot upgrade of a release needs appup and relup files. Few teams use it in production.
* Most teams deploy new nodes with a rolling deploy, like a Go service.
* In development, Phoenix code reloading and IEx `recompile/0` use the same feature every day.
